## 5. Existing duplicate-detection tests

### /home/daniel/sandbox/src/test/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/acceptance/AcceptanceTest.java (full file)
```java
package org.springframework.samples.petclinic.acceptance;

import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;
import static org.springframework.test.web.servlet.result.MockMvcResultMatchers.status;
import tools.jackson.databind.node.ObjectNode;

/** identity-key: Consolidate all duplicate detection into a single derived 'identityKey' = normalizedTeleph... */
class AcceptanceTest extends AcceptanceBase {

	@Test
	void coreRejectsIdentityCollision() throws Exception {
		ObjectNode a = ownerNode();
		createOwnerOk(a);
		ObjectNode b = ownerNode();
		b.put("telephone", a.get("telephone").asText());
		createOwner(b).andExpect(status().isConflict()); // same identityKey
	}
}
```
The class-level comment is the (truncated) task marker: "Consolidate all duplicate detection into a single derived 'identityKey' = normalizedTeleph…". The only test asserts that reusing another owner's telephone yields 409.

### /home/daniel/sandbox/src/test/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/acceptance/AcceptanceBase.java (test scaffolding)
Relevant helpers: `ownerNode()` (lines 117–125) builds a fully-unique valid owner (firstName "Test", unique lastName/address/city/telephone; no email); `uniqueTelephone()` → `"04%08d"` (Australian, normalizes to `+61…`); `uniqueEmail()`; `createOwner(body)` POSTs to `/api/owners`; `createOwnerOk(body)` expects 2xx and returns the id; `createOwnerWithKey` adds an `Idempotency-Key` header. Also `sha256hex`/`shaHex` helpers (lines ~282–297) exist — likely useful for asserting a derived householdId/identityKey. No existing tests assert email or household 409s directly.

---

## 6. Exception classes (duplicates / conflicts)

All three extend `java.lang.Exception`, carry the offending value(s), and are mapped to **409 CONFLICT** by their handlers, which return `Map.of("errors", List.of(<field(s)>))`.

- **/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/escalation/DuplicateTelephoneException.java** — ctor `DuplicateTelephoneException(String telephone)`, `getTelephone()`.
- **/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/escalation/DuplicateEmailException.java** — ctor `DuplicateEmailException(String email)`, `getEmail()`.
- **/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/escalation/DuplicateHouseholdException.java** — ctor `DuplicateHouseholdException(String lastName, String address)`, `getLastName()`, `getAddress()`.

Handlers (each 3-line `handle` method):
- **DuplicateTelephoneExceptionHandler.java** → `409`, body `{"errors":["telephone"]}`
- **DuplicateEmailExceptionHandler.java** → `409`, body `{"errors":["email"]}`
- **DuplicateHouseholdExceptionHandler.java** → `409`, body `{"errors":["lastName","address"]}`

All three handlers use signature `handle(@Parameter <Ex> ex, ObjectResponse<ResponseEntity<Map<String, List<String>>>> response)` and `response.send(ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.CONFLICT).body(...))`.

Related non-duplicate exceptions in the same package (context): `MissingOwnerFieldsException` (+handler, 400), `InvalidTelephoneException`, `InvalidEmailException`.

---

## Key takeaways for making changes toward `identityKey`
- Three separate check functions + three exception/handler pairs currently implement duplicate detection, wired only into `owners.POST.yml`.
- Normalization logic already exists and is reused: `TelephoneE164.normalize`, email lower-case (in `ValidateOwner` and `CheckEmailUnique`), and `AddressNormalizer.normalize` + last-name collapse (in `CheckHouseholdUnique`/`AssignHousehold`).
- Neither the `Owner` entity nor the OpenAPI DTOs currently have an `identityKey` field.
- The PUT path has no duplicate checks at all.
- The sole acceptance test (`coreRejectsIdentityCollision`) already frames the desired behavior as a single `identityKey` collision returning 409.

